<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<model version="NetLogo 7.0.4" snapToGrid="true">
  <code><![CDATA[breed [ resources resource ]
breed [ foragers forager ]

foragers-own [
  R1-density                                                          ; Generalized metric of local resource density at current location of forager; only calculated for giving-up density strategy and after resource consumption or after step length completion
  R2-density
  both-density
  search-mode                                                         ; Forager can be in one of 4 behavioral modes: extensive search (random), intensive search (random), direct movement (move directly to known resource location), and handling (not moving)
  step-length                                                         ; Length of step drawn by forager from distribution specified by Levy exponent
  last-handle-counter
  last-resource-handle
  ]

resources-own [
  generation                                                          ; Generation of resource - parent or offspring; used in distributing resources according to Neyman-Scott process (constrained by total number of resources)
  resource-type
  energy
  handle
  rejected?                                                           ; A switch to mark a resource as recently rejected
  R1-neighbors
  R2-neighbors
  both-neighbors
  ]

patches-own [
  R1-field                                                            ; Generalized measure of local resource density; calculated at center of patch; computationally expensive, so only calculated for visualization
  R2-field
  both-field
  ]

globals [
  cluster-radius                                                      ; Larger of the cluster radii for the two resource types; used for visualizing landscape set-up
  forager-count
  handling-time
  total-dist                                                          ; Total distance moved by all foragers
  energy-gained                                                       ; Total energy gained by all foragers
  resource-rejections                                                 ; Total number of R2 resources rejected
  rejected-resources                                                  ; Agentset of resources currently marked as rejected; lets go check only these instead of all resources
  step-num                                                            ; Number of steps drawn by all foragers
  death-times
  type-eaten
  R1-neighbor-list
  R2-neighbor-list
  both-neighbor-list
  ]

to setup
  clear-all                                                           ; Resets all global variables to zero, and clears turtles, patches, drawing, plots, and output
  set-globals                                                         ; Initializes values of global variables
  color-landscape                                                     ; Sets color of landscape for visualization
  add-parents
  add-offspring
  ask resources with [generation = "parent"][die]
  repeat forager-num [add-forager]                                    ; Adds foragers to the landscape - preliminary tests indicates that, for example, 1 forager searching for 1000 time steps is the same as 10 foragers searching for 100 time steps
  set forager-count (count foragers)
  if (calculate-neighbors?)[
    calculate-R1-neighbors
    calculate-R2-neighbors
    calculate-both-neighbors
    ]
  reset-ticks
end

to set-globals
  set handling-time 0
  set energy-gained 0
  set step-num 0
  set total-dist 0
  set resource-rejections 0
  set rejected-resources no-turtles
  ifelse (R1-radius >= R2-radius)[set cluster-radius R1-radius][set cluster-radius R2-radius]
  set death-times [ ]
  set type-eaten [ ]
  set R1-neighbor-list [ ]
  set R2-neighbor-list [ ]
  set both-neighbor-list [ ]
end

; Landscape is 113x113 patches (i.e., grid cells) - outer boundary (red patches) absorbs foragers that move onto it leaving 111x111 area for foragers to move throughout
to color-landscape
  ask patches[
    if ( pxcor >= (-50 - cluster-radius) and pxcor <= (50 + cluster-radius) and pycor >= (-50 - cluster-radius) and pycor <= (50 + cluster-radius))  ; max extent of area occupied by parent resources; used for Neyman-Scott process
      [set pcolor blue]
    if ( pxcor >= -56 and pxcor <= 56 and pycor >= -56 and pycor <= 56)                                                                              ; absorbing boundary
      [set pcolor red]
    if ( pxcor >= -55 and pxcor <= 55 and pycor >= -55 and pycor <= 55)                                                                              ; buffer
      [set pcolor yellow]
    if ( pxcor >= -50 and pxcor <= 50 and pycor >= -50 and pycor <= 50)                                                                              ; core area
      [set pcolor green]
    ]
end

to add-parents
  set-default-shape resources "dot"                                     ; Set shape of resources to dot - foragers and resources are actually points, but can be given arbitrary dimensions for visualization
  while [count resources with [resource-type = "R1"] = 0][
    create-resources random-poisson R1-clusters [
      setxy ((random-float (101 + 2 * R1-radius)) - (50.5 + R1-radius)) ((random-float (101 + 2 * R1-radius)) - (50.5 + R1-radius))
      set color orange
      set size 1.5
      set generation "parent"
      set resource-type "R1"
      set energy R1-energy
      set handle R1-handle
      set rejected? false
      ]
    ]
  while [count resources with [resource-type = "R2"] = 0][
    create-resources random-poisson R2-clusters [
      setxy ((random-float (101 + 2 * R2-radius)) - (50.5 + R2-radius)) ((random-float (101 + 2 * R2-radius)) - (50.5 + R2-radius))
      set color white
      set size 1.5
      set generation "parent"
      set resource-type "R2"
      set energy R2-energy
      set handle R2-handle
      set rejected? false
      ]
    ]
end

to add-offspring
  set-default-shape resources "dot"                                     ; Set shape of resources to dot - foragers and resources are actually points, but can be given arbitrary dimensions for visualization
  let R1-placed 0                                                     ; Number of R1 offspring that landed in the core area; avoids recounting resources on every pass
  while [R1-placed < R1-num][
    ask one-of resources with [generation = "parent" and resource-type = "R1"][
      hatch-resources 1 [
      set color black
      set generation "offspring"
      rt random 360
      fd (random-float R1-radius)
      ifelse ( [pcolor] of patch-here != green)[die][set R1-placed (R1-placed + 1)]
      ]
    ]
  ]
  let R2-placed 0                                                     ; Number of R2 offspring that landed in the core area; avoids recounting resources on every pass
  while [R2-placed < R2-num][
    ask one-of resources with [generation = "parent" and resource-type = "R2"][
      hatch-resources 1 [
      set color red
      set generation "offspring"
      rt random 360
      fd (random-float R2-radius)
      ifelse ( [pcolor] of patch-here != green)[die][set R2-placed (R2-placed + 1)]
      ]
    ]
  ]
end

; Foragers are distributed through a 101x101 patch area centered in the larger 111x111 area according to a random uniform distribution
; Foragers can move through a buffer zone (5 patches wide on all sides) before encountering the landscape boundary
to add-forager
  set-default-shape foragers "dot"                                     ; Set shape of resources to dot - foragers and resources are actually points, but can be given arbitrary dimensions for visualization
  create-foragers 1 [                                                  ; Creates a single forager at a time - looping to create multiple foragers occurs, as needed, in setup procedure
    set color white
    setxy ((random-float 101) - 50.5) ((random-float 101) - 50.5)      ; Choose random coordinate for forager within core area
    set step-length 0                                                  ; Initialize forager's step length to zero indicating that a new step length needs to be drawn
    if (PD?)[pen-down]                                                 ; If true, the forager's path will be traced - visualization purposes only
  ]
end

to go
   while [count foragers < forager-num][
     set forager-count (forager-count + 1)
     add-forager                                                       ; Adds a new forager if one of the previous foragers has been absorbed by the boundary
     ]
   ask foragers [
     ifelse(last-handle-counter >= last-resource-handle)[
       let nearby-resources (resources in-radius 0.01) with [not rejected?] ; Filter by radius first so NetLogo only checks nearby resources
       ifelse (any? nearby-resources)[
         recognize-resource min-one-of nearby-resources [distance myself]
         ][
         set-target
         move
         ]
       ][
       handle-resource
       ]
     if ( [pcolor] of patch-here = red)[die]                          ; If the forager's movements take it to the landscape boundary, then it is removed from the population (absorbing boundary), and replaced the next time through the go procedure
     ]
   tick                                                                ; Advance the tick counter
   if (not any? resources) [ stop ]                                    ; Stops run of simulation if no resources on landscape
   ask rejected-resources [if (not any? foragers in-radius perceptual-radius)[set rejected? false]]
   set rejected-resources rejected-resources with [rejected?]          ; Drop resources that are available again
end

to recognize-resource [closest-resource]                              ; closest-resource is the nearest unrejected resource, found in go
  ifelse ([resource-type] of closest-resource = "R1")[
    consume-resource closest-resource
    ][
    if(Selective?)[calculate-R1-density]
    ifelse (Selective? and R1-density > rejection-density)[
      ask closest-resource[set rejected? true]
      set rejected-resources (turtle-set rejected-resources closest-resource)
      set resource-rejections (resource-rejections + 1)
      set step-length 0                                                  ; Encounters with resources reset step length and heading; cost of resource recognition (1 time step and reset step); forager doesn't just cruise through areas with low-quality resources
      set-target
      ][
      consume-resource closest-resource
      ]
    ]
end

to consume-resource [closest-resource]
  set last-resource-handle [handle] of closest-resource
  set energy-gained (energy-gained + [energy] of closest-resource)
  set type-eaten lput ([resource-type] of closest-resource) type-eaten
  set R1-neighbor-list lput ([R1-neighbors] of closest-resource) R1-neighbor-list
  set R2-neighbor-list lput ([R2-neighbors] of closest-resource) R2-neighbor-list
  set both-neighbor-list lput ([both-neighbors] of closest-resource) both-neighbor-list
  set death-times lput ticks death-times
  ask closest-resource [die]                                           ; consume resource
  set search-mode "handling"
  set last-handle-counter 1
  set handling-time (handling-time + 1)
end

to handle-resource
  set last-handle-counter (last-handle-counter + 1)
  set handling-time (handling-time + 1)
end

to set-target
  let available-resources no-turtles
  if (search-mode != "direct")[                                       ; Skip the search when already moving directly to a resource
    set available-resources (resources in-radius perceptual-radius) with [not rejected?]  ; Filter by radius first so NetLogo only checks nearby resources
    ]
  ifelse (any? available-resources)[
    let closest-resource min-one-of available-resources [distance myself]       ; Nearest resource within the perceptual radius
    face closest-resource                                             ; set the heading of the forager to move directly to the nearest resource, and
    set step-length distance closest-resource                         ; set the step-length of the forager to move the exact distance to the nearest resource
    set search-mode "direct"
    set step-num (step-num + 1)                                       ; increment counter of number of steps drawn
    ][
    if (step-length <= 0)[
      set step-num (step-num + 1)                                     ; increment counter of number of steps drawn
      rt random 360                                                          ; turn right to orient to that heading
      ifelse (Both-GUD?) [
        calculate-both-density
        compare-both-GUD
        ][
        calculate-R1-density
        compare-R1-GUD
        ]
     ]
  ]
end

to compare-both-GUD                                                      ; Compares local resource density to threshold value (i.e., giving-up density)
  ifelse (both-density > giving-up-density )[                        ; If the local resource density > giving-up density
    set search-mode "intensive"                                              ; then search mode is set to intensive
    set step-length levy intensive-mu
    ][
    set search-mode "extensive"                                              ; else the search mode is set to extensive
    set step-length levy extensive-mu
    ]
end

to compare-R1-GUD                                                        ; Compares local resource density to threshold value (i.e., giving-up density)
  ifelse (R1-density > giving-up-density )[                        ; If the local resource density > giving-up density
    set search-mode "intensive"                                              ; then search mode is set to intensive
    set step-length levy intensive-mu
    ][
    set search-mode "extensive"                                              ; else the search mode is set to extensive
    set step-length levy extensive-mu
    ]
end

to calculate-R1-density                                             ; Local R1 density at the forager's current location (only calculated after resource consumption or after completing a step)
  set R1-density (R1-energy / R1-handle) * kernel-sum resources with [resource-type = "R1"]
end

to calculate-R2-density                                             ; Local R2 density at the forager's current location (only calculated after resource consumption or after completing a step)
  set R2-density (R2-energy / R2-handle) * kernel-sum resources with [resource-type = "R2"]
end

to calculate-both-density                                             ; Same process as in visualize-gradient procedure, but saves computational time by only calculating the resource gradient for patches at the current location of the forager (and only after resource consumption or after completing a step)
  calculate-R1-density
  calculate-R2-density
  set both-density (R1-density + R2-density)
end

to calculate-R1-neighbors                                            ; Gaussian-weighted number of R1 resources around each resource (excluding itself)
  ask resources [ set R1-neighbors kernel-sum other resources with [resource-type = "R1"] ]
end

to calculate-R2-neighbors                                            ; Gaussian-weighted number of R2 resources around each resource (excluding itself)
  ask resources [ set R2-neighbors kernel-sum other resources with [resource-type = "R2"] ]
end

to calculate-both-neighbors
  ask resources [ set both-neighbors (R1-neighbors + R2-neighbors) ]
end

to-report kernel-sum [agents]                                        ; Sum of Gaussian kernel weights from the calling agent to each agent in agents - resources influence density globally, but the weight of each contribution decays with distance as a normal distribution
  let two-sigma-sq 2 * sigma ^ 2
  report (1 / (sqrt (2 * pi) * sigma)) * sum [exp (0 - (distance myself) ^ 2 / two-sigma-sq)] of agents
end

to move                                                                ; Incrementally move the distance of the step length
  ifelse(step-length < speed)[                                         ; If the step length is less than the speed
      fd step-length                                                       ; forager moves forward distance of step length
      set total-dist (total-dist + step-length)
      set step-length 0                                                    ; step length has been completed - new step length will be drawn in next time step (unless forager has detected resource)
    ][
      fd speed                                                             ; forager moves forward distance specified by speed
      set total-dist (total-dist + speed)
      set step-length (step-length - speed)                                ; update distance remaining to be moved by forager along this step
    ]
end

to-report levy[mu]                                                     ; Report step length based on Levy exponent - following Viswanathan et al. Nature 1999
   ifelse(mu <= 1)[
      report random-float 100000000000                                 ; In the limit of mu to 1, the pareto distribution approaches an infinite uniform disitrubition
      ][
      ifelse(mu >= 3)[
         report abs (random-normal 0 1)                                ; In the limit of mu to 3, the pareto distribution approaches a normal distribution
         ][
         let a random-float 1
         report perceptual-radius * exp(ln(a) * (1 / (1 - mu)))        ; Draws step length from pareto distribution with specified exponent - minimum step length is set by perceptual radius
         ]
     ]
end

to visualize-R1-field                                            ; Calculates resource field for all patches on the landscape (except for the patches in the outer boundary) and scales the color of the patch to the value of the resource field
  let arena patches with [pxcor >= -55 and pxcor <= 55 and pycor >= -55 and pycor <= 55]
  let R1s resources with [resource-type = "R1"]
  ask arena [ set R1-field (R1-energy / R1-handle) * kernel-sum R1s ]   ; Resource field is calculated at the center of each patch
  let rf-max max ([R1-field] of arena)                         ; Color gradient is set based on the maximum gradient value
  ask arena[set pcolor scale-color (green) R1-field 0 rf-max]          ; lighter colors indicate higher values
end

to visualize-both-field                                            ; Calculates resource field for all patches on the landscape (except for the patches in the outer boundary) and scales the color of the patch to the value of the resource field
  let arena patches with [pxcor >= -55 and pxcor <= 55 and pycor >= -55 and pycor <= 55]
  let R1s resources with [resource-type = "R1"]
  let R2s resources with [resource-type = "R2"]
  ask arena [                                                      ; Resource field is calculated at the center of each patch
    set R1-field (R1-energy / R1-handle) * kernel-sum R1s
    set R2-field (R2-energy / R2-handle) * kernel-sum R2s
    set both-field (R1-field + R2-field)
    ]
  let rf-max max ([both-field] of arena)                         ; Color gradient is set based on the maximum gradient value
  ask arena[set pcolor scale-color (green) both-field 0 rf-max]          ; lighter colors indicate higher values
end]]></code>
  <widgets>
    <view x="220" wrappingAllowedX="false" y="25" frameRate="30.0" minPycor="-114" height="925" showTickCounter="true" patchSize="4.0" fontSize="10" wrappingAllowedY="false" width="924" tickCounterLabel="ticks" maxPycor="114" updateMode="1" maxPxcor="114" minPxcor="-114"></view>
    <note x="15" y="15" backgroundDark="0" fontSize="14" width="190" markdown="false" height="25" textColorDark="-1" textColorLight="-16777216" backgroundLight="0">Resources</note>
    <slider x="15" step="100" y="45" max="300" display="R1-num" height="50" min="100" direction="Horizontal" default="125.0" variable="R1-num" width="190"></slider>
    <slider x="15" step="1" y="100" max="20" display="R1-clusters" height="50" min="1" direction="Horizontal" default="15.0" variable="R1-clusters" width="190"></slider>
    <slider x="15" step="56" y="155" max="64" display="R1-radius" height="50" min="8" direction="Horizontal" default="64.0" variable="R1-radius" width="190"></slider>
    <slider x="15" step="90" y="210" max="100" display="R1-energy" height="50" min="10" direction="Horizontal" default="100.0" variable="R1-energy" width="190"></slider>
    <slider x="15" step="990" y="265" max="1000" display="R1-handle" height="50" min="10" direction="Horizontal" default="10.0" variable="R1-handle" width="190"></slider>
    <slider x="15" step="100" y="335" max="300" display="R2-num" height="50" min="100" direction="Horizontal" default="375.0" variable="R2-num" width="190"></slider>
    <slider x="15" step="1" y="390" max="20" display="R2-clusters" height="50" min="1" direction="Horizontal" default="15.0" variable="R2-clusters" width="190"></slider>
    <slider x="15" step="56" y="445" max="64" display="R2-radius" height="50" min="8" direction="Horizontal" default="64.0" variable="R2-radius" width="190"></slider>
    <slider x="15" step="90" y="500" max="100" display="R2-energy" height="50" min="10" direction="Horizontal" default="100.0" variable="R2-energy" width="190"></slider>
    <slider x="15" step="990" y="555" max="1000" display="R2-handle" height="50" min="10" direction="Horizontal" default="1000.0" variable="R2-handle" width="190"></slider>
    <switch x="15" y="625" height="40" on="true" variable="calculate-neighbors?" display="calculate-neighbors?" width="190"></switch>
    <button x="1160" y="15" height="40" disableUntilTicks="false" forever="false" kind="Observer" width="90">setup</button>
    <button x="1260" y="15" height="40" disableUntilTicks="false" forever="true" kind="Observer" width="90">go</button>
    <note x="1160" y="70" backgroundDark="0" fontSize="14" width="190" markdown="false" height="25" textColorDark="-1" textColorLight="-16777216" backgroundLight="0">Foragers</note>
    <slider x="1160" step="1" y="100" max="10" display="forager-num" height="50" min="1" direction="Horizontal" default="1.0" variable="forager-num" width="190"></slider>
    <slider x="1160" step="0.05" y="155" max="2" display="perceptual-radius" height="50" min="0.1" direction="Horizontal" default="0.5" variable="perceptual-radius" width="190"></slider>
    <slider x="1160" step="0.05" y="210" max="1" display="speed" height="50" min="0.05" direction="Horizontal" default="0.25" variable="speed" width="190"></slider>
    <slider x="1160" step="0.25" y="265" max="5" display="sigma" height="50" min="0.25" direction="Horizontal" default="1.0" variable="sigma" width="190"></slider>
    <slider x="1160" step="0.1" y="335" max="3" display="extensive-mu" height="50" min="1" direction="Horizontal" default="1.0" variable="extensive-mu" width="190"></slider>
    <slider x="1160" step="0.1" y="390" max="3" display="intensive-mu" height="50" min="1" direction="Horizontal" default="3.0" variable="intensive-mu" width="190"></slider>
    <slider x="1160" step="0.001" y="445" max="1" display="giving-up-density" height="50" min="0" direction="Horizontal" default="0.01" variable="giving-up-density" width="190"></slider>
    <switch x="1160" y="500" height="40" on="true" variable="Both-GUD?" display="Both-GUD?" width="190"></switch>
    <slider x="1160" step="0.01" y="555" max="100" display="rejection-density" height="50" min="0" direction="Horizontal" default="100.0" variable="rejection-density" width="190"></slider>
    <switch x="1160" y="610" height="40" on="false" variable="Selective?" display="Selective?" width="190"></switch>
    <switch x="1160" y="670" height="40" on="false" variable="PD?" display="PD?" width="190"></switch>
  </widgets>
  <info>## WHAT IS IT?

This model extends the composite random search model of Nolting et al. (2015) by adding a second resource type. It asks how foragers that use composite random search behave when the landscape holds two clustered resources that differ in energy value and handling time. It also asks whether choosing which resources to eat changes what foragers gain.

The model is aimed at shared predation: indirect interactions between two resource types that are eaten by the same forager. Because foragers eat both R1 and R2, the abundance, arrangement and quality of one resource type can change how heavily the other is eaten. These indirect effects can be negative (apparent competition; Holt 1977) or positive (apparent mutualism; Abrams and Matsuda 1996). Forager numbers are fixed in this model, so it captures only the short-term effects that arise from forager behavior, such as handling time and diet choice, and not the long-term effects of changes in forager numbers.

Because resources are clustered in space, these effects can also appear as neighborhood (associational) effects, where a resource's risk of being eaten depends on what is around it. In *shared doom*, also called associational susceptibility, living near a preferred resource raises the chance of being eaten (Wahl and Hay 1995; Emerson et al. 2012). In an *associational refuge*, also called associational resistance, neighbors lower that chance (Hay 1986; Barbosa et al. 2009). Emerson et al. (2012) showed that which effect appears depends on the consumer's diet choice and on the spatial scale at which the resources are mixed.

The model is unfinished and exploratory.

## HOW IT WORKS

### Landscape

The landscape uses colored zones:

- **Green core**, from -50 to 50: resources are kept only here, and foragers start here.
- **Yellow buffer**, out to ±55: foragers can move here.
- **Red boundary**, at ±56: absorbs foragers. A forager that steps onto a red patch is removed and replaced by a new forager.
- **Blue band**, outside the red boundary: shows how far cluster centers can sit outside the core. It appears only when the larger cluster radius is more than 6.

Each resource type, R1 and R2, is placed using a Neyman-Scott process:

1. The number of cluster centers (parents) is drawn from a Poisson distribution with mean `R1-clusters` or `R2-clusters`. Parents are placed uniformly in a square that extends one cluster radius beyond the core.
2. Offspring are created one at a time near a randomly chosen parent of the same type, at a random direction and at a uniform random distance up to `R1-radius` or `R2-radius`. Offspring that land outside the green core are discarded. This repeats until there are `R1-num` or `R2-num` offspring.
3. The parents are removed, so only offspring remain as resources.

R1 resources are black and R2 resources are red.

### Local resource density

Foragers sense local resource density as a Gaussian-weighted sum over all resources, where σ is `sigma`:

    density = Σ (1 / (√(2π) σ)) × (energy / handle) × exp(−d² / (2σ²))

Here `d` is the distance from the forager to each resource. Each resource is weighted by how profitable it is, which is its energy divided by its handling time.

### Forager behavior

Each tick, each forager does one of the following:

- **Handling.** If the forager is still handling its last resource, it stays put. A resource takes `handle` ticks to handle.
- **At a resource.** If the forager is within 0.01 of a resource it has not rejected, it recognizes the resource:
  - It always eats an R1 resource.
  - It eats an R2 resource unless `Selective?` is on and local R1 density is greater than `rejection-density`. In that case, it rejects the R2 resource and draws a new step. A rejected resource becomes available again once no forager is within the perceptual radius.
- **Direct movement.** If an available resource is within the perceptual radius (`perceptual-radius`), the forager turns toward the nearest one and moves straight to it.
- **Random search.** Otherwise, when its current step is finished, the forager turns to a random heading and calculates local resource density. This is R1 density by default, or the combined density of R1 and R2 if `Both-GUD?` is on. It then draws a new step length:
  - If density is greater than `giving-up-density`, it uses **intensive search** (`intensive-mu`).
  - Otherwise, it uses **extensive search** (`extensive-mu`).

Foragers move at most `speed` patches per tick. Keep `speed` smaller than `perceptual-radius` (the defaults are 0.25 and 0.5) so that a forager never steps over a resource without seeing it.

### Step lengths

Step lengths are drawn from a Lévy (Pareto) distribution with exponent μ:

- μ ≤ 1: uniform between 0 and 10^11, which approximates ballistic movement.
- μ ≥ 3: the absolute value of a standard normal draw, which approximates Brownian movement.
- Otherwise: a Pareto draw whose minimum step length is the perceptual radius.

The simulation stops when no resources are left.

## HOW TO USE IT

Set the parameters, click **setup**, then click **go**.

Resource parameters are in the left column, and forager parameters are in the right column.

- `R1-num` / `R2-num`: number of resources of each type.
- `R1-clusters` / `R2-clusters`: mean number of clusters (Poisson).
- `R1-radius` / `R2-radius`: cluster radius. The slider switches between 8 (tightly clustered) and 64 (nearly uniform).
- `R1-energy` / `R2-energy`: energy gained from eating one resource. The slider switches between 10 and 100.
- `R1-handle` / `R2-handle`: handling time in ticks. The slider switches between 10 and 1000.
- `calculate-neighbors?`: at setup, calculates how many R1 and R2 neighbors each resource has, weighted by distance. Eaten resources record these values. Its cost grows with the square of the number of resources.

Forager parameters (right column):

- `forager-num`: number of foragers on the landscape at once.
- `perceptual-radius`: distance at which a forager detects a resource and moves straight to it. It is also the minimum Lévy step length.
- `speed`: maximum distance a forager moves per tick. Keep it smaller than `perceptual-radius`.
- `sigma`: width (σ) of the Gaussian kernel used for local resource density and neighbor calculations.
- `extensive-mu` / `intensive-mu`: Lévy exponents for extensive and intensive search.
- `giving-up-density`: density threshold for switching between search modes.
- `Both-GUD?`: bases the giving-up decision on combined R1 and R2 density instead of R1 density alone.
- `rejection-density`: R1 density above which a selective forager rejects R2.
- `Selective?`: lets foragers reject R2 resources.
- `PD?`: draws forager paths.

The model records these outputs, which you can read in the Command Center or in BehaviorSpace:

- `total-dist`: total distance moved by all foragers.
- `energy-gained`: total energy gained.
- `handling-time`: total ticks spent handling.
- `resource-rejections`: number of R2 resources rejected.
- `step-num`: number of steps drawn.
- `forager-count`: total number of foragers created, including replacements.
- `death-times`: tick at which each resource was eaten.
- `type-eaten`: type of each resource eaten.
- `R1-neighbor-list`, `R2-neighbor-list`, `both-neighbor-list`: neighborhood density of each resource eaten.

To color patches by the resource density field, run `visualize-R1-field` or `visualize-both-field` in the Command Center.

## THINGS TO NOTICE

- Watch how foragers switch between long extensive steps and short intensive steps as they enter and leave clusters.
- Compare the energy gained when the two resources are clustered differently, for example when R1 is tightly clustered and R2 is spread out.
- Check whether R2 resources near R1 clusters are eaten more often than isolated R2 resources. Dense R1 triggers intensive search, so nearby R2 may suffer shared doom. With `Selective?` on, foragers reject R2 where R1 is dense, so R1 may instead give nearby R2 an associational refuge.

## THINGS TO TRY

- Turn on `Selective?` and vary `rejection-density` to see when rejecting R2 pays off.
- Compare giving-up decisions based only on R1 with decisions based on both resources (`Both-GUD?`).
- Vary the number, clustering, energy or handling time of R2 and see how much R1 is eaten. This shows how one resource affects the other through the shared forager.
- Vary `R1-radius` and `R2-radius` to change how closely the two resources overlap in space, and see whether shared doom or associational refuge changes with the scale of mixing.
- Use the included BehaviorSpace experiments to sweep cluster radius, energy, handling time and selectivity.
- Run the `Replacement` and `Additive` experiments to compare the two standard designs for associational effects ([Hambäck et al. 2014](https://doi.org/10.1890/13-0793.1)). `Replacement` holds total resources at 500 and varies the R1:R2 mix from 0:500 to 500:0. `Additive` holds one type at 250 and adds 0 to 500 of the other, with each type taking a turn as the focal type. Compare the fraction of each type eaten with its monoculture run (other type = 0). Hambäck et al. predict that replacement designs mostly show shared doom and additive designs mostly show associational refuge.

## EXTENDING THE MODEL

- Make forager traits, such as perceptual radius, speed and σ, into per-forager variables so that foragers can differ.
- Let foragers interact with each other, for example by competing for the same resources.

## NETLOGO FEATURES

- Resources and foragers are two breeds that share the same space.
- The density calculations sum over every resource, so their cost grows with the number of resources. They use `sum [...] of` and `in-radius` instead of explicit loops, which NetLogo runs much faster.
- The world extends to ±114 so that clusters with the largest radius (64) can have centers outside the 101 × 101 core. Most of the space outside the absorbing boundary is used only during setup.

## RELATED MODELS

- Composite random search model: https://github.com/hinkelman/composite-random-search

## CREDITS AND REFERENCES

Abrams, P. A., and Matsuda, H. (1996). Positive indirect effects between prey species that share predators. Ecology, 77, 610–616. https://doi.org/10.2307/2265634

Barbosa, P., Hines, J., Kaplan, I., Martinson, H., Szczepaniec, A., and Szendrei, Z. (2009). Associational resistance and associational susceptibility: having right or wrong neighbors. Annual Review of Ecology, Evolution, and Systematics, 40, 1–20. https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev.ecolsys.110308.120242

Emerson, S. E., Brown, J. S., Whelan, C. J., and Schmidt, K. A. (2012). Scale-dependent neighborhood effects: shared doom and associational refuge. Oecologia, 168, 659–670. https://doi.org/10.1007/s00442-011-2144-4

Hay, M. E. (1986). Associational plant defenses and the maintenance of species diversity: turning competitors into accomplices. The American Naturalist, 128, 617–641. https://doi.org/10.1086/284593

Holt, R. D. (1977). Predation, apparent competition, and the structure of prey communities. Theoretical Population Biology, 12, 197–229. https://doi.org/10.1016/0040-5809(77)90042-9

Nolting, B. C., Hinkelman, T. M., Brassil, C. E., and Tenhumberg, B. (2015). Composite random search strategies based on non-directional sensory cues. Ecological Complexity, 22, 126–138. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ecocom.2015.03.002

Wahl, M., and Hay, M. E. (1995). Associational resistance and shared doom: effects of epibiosis on herbivory. Oecologia, 102, 329–340. https://doi.org/10.1007/BF00329800

Source: https://github.com/hinkelman/shared-predation</info>
